# Package update @VERSION@ (signed repo @REPO@): no reinstall

Internal test release for the CyberHygiene Project development team. Not an official Rocky Linux product (`NOTICE.txt`).

## What it fixes

**`chp-integrity` 0.1.5.** The AIDE exclusions in 0.2.0 matched as name *prefixes*. So, for example, the exclusion for
`/root/chp-escrow-pending` also hid a lookalike path such as `/root/chp-escrow-pendingX/`. The same was true for the
Kanidm certificate, step-ca database and AIDE log folders.

The exclusions now cover only their own folder and its contents, and every lookalike path is checked. Only someone who
already has root could have used the gap.

Nothing else changes. Every other package in this repo is the same build as in 0.2.0.

## Apply it (each 0.2.0 host, server first)

1. **Check this folder** as `0.2.0/VERIFY.md` describes: `SHA256SUMS.asc` against the project key, then
   `shasum -a 256 -c SHA256SUMS`.
2. Copy `chp-repo-@REPO@.tar.gz` to the host, then unpack it over the local repo as root:

       rm -rf /var/lib/chp/repo/*
       tar -xzf chp-repo-@REPO@.tar.gz -C /var/lib/chp/repo --strip-components=1

3. Upgrade:

       dnf --enablerepo=chp upgrade chp-integrity

## Expect one AIDE alert, then accept it

The update replaces `/etc/aide.conf`, so the next daily check reports it. In the `aide-accept` summary, the package's
own files are listed under "Explained by signed package updates". **`/etc/aide.conf` is listed under "NOT explained"**,
because the package installs it by copying its own copy into place.

Before accepting, confirm it is exactly the packaged file:

    cmp /etc/aide.conf /usr/share/chp-integrity/aide.conf && echo SAME
    rpm -V chp-integrity && echo PACKAGE-OK

If both print, accept the change as the ISSO or a named delegate:

    chp-site aide-accept --approver "Your Name" --reason "chp-integrity 0.1.5 update (@VERSION@): anchored exclusions"

If either check fails, **do not accept.** Investigate first: the file was changed by something other than the update.
